# HQL-0063: Paths a program reads: `/` between segments on every host

**Status**: implemented in part
**Created**: 2026-11-22
**Issues**: [0082](../../issues/0082-windows-paths-a-program-reads.hmd)
**Source**: the first build and test of `hql` on Windows, at `v0.7.1`; the
`Path` of [[HQL-0040]], the sorted walk of [[HQL-0020]] and the source
directory of [[HQL-0059]]

## Abstract

A path a program reads is text with `/` between its segments, on every host.
The form the host writes a path in, `\` between segments and `\\?\` before a
canonical path on Windows, is kept inside the runtime and is converted where a
path becomes a value. One function converts it. A card's path, an entry of a
listing, `fs.source_dir()`, `fs.working_dir()` and `vault.root` are that text.
The roots a `Path` knows are `/`, a drive root `C:/` and a share
`//server/share`, read from the text alone.

## Motivation

[[HQL-0040]] makes a `Path` text and its operations pure: `join`, `parent`,
`name`, `stem` and `extension` read the text and never the file system, and
they read `/` as the separator. It does not say what text a path the host
gives is. On Linux and macOS the host writes `/` too, so the two agreed
without a rule. On Windows they do not, and one location has three texts:

| Form | Text |
| --- | --- |
| as Windows writes it | `D:\a\hql\examples\projects\library\lib\shelf` |
| canonical | `\\?\D:\a\hql\examples\projects\library\lib\shelf` |
| as a program reads it | `D:/a/hql/examples/projects/library/lib/shelf` |

The canonical form is what `std::fs::canonicalize` returns on Windows: an
extended-length path, in which Windows reads `/` as a character of a name and
not as a separator. It is the host's form and not an escaping error.

The first run of the tests on Windows showed the host's form reaching programs
in two places ([issue 0082](../../issues/0082-windows-paths-a-program-reads.hmd)):

- A card below a folder had the path `books\a-room-of-ones-own.hmd`, so
  `cards | filter(c => c.path | starts_with("books/")) | size` was `0` where
  it is `7` on Linux and macOS. A vault did not read the same on two machines.
- `fs.source_dir().name` was the whole canonical path, where it is `shelf`
  elsewhere: the text holds no `/` for `name` to find.

Both are the runtime handing a program the host's text. The rule that was
missing is stated here, so the next path that becomes a value has one place to
go through.

## Non-goals

- The text of a path a message names. A diagnostic, `hql context` and an
  error of the host name a path as the host writes it.
- Case. Windows and macOS usually read `Books` and `books` as one folder; a
  `Path` compares by its text, as it does today.
- Line endings of a card saved on Windows.

## Specification

### 1. The invariant

- A path the runtime gives a program MUST be written with `/` between its
  segments on every host, and MUST NOT open with `\\?\`. A program, a card and
  an example are then the same text on every machine, and `Path`'s operations
  find the segments.
- A program MUST NOT need a host-specific form to name a file. `file("data/birds.csv")`
  and `folder("C:/Users/ada/notes")` name the same file on Windows as the
  host's own spelling does; Windows reads `/` as a separator in every path
  that does not open with `\\?\`.

### 2. One conversion, at the boundary

- The runtime MUST keep a host path in the host's form, a `PathBuf`, wherever
  it opens, lists or compares files.
- A host path MUST become a value through one function, `paths::of`. No other
  code writes a host path into a `String` a program reads.
- The conversion is:

```text
of(path):
    text = the host's text of path
    on Windows:
        "\\?\X:\rest"            ->  "X:\rest"            X a drive letter
        "\\?\UNC\server\share…"  ->  "\\server\share…"
        any other "\\?\…"        ->  unchanged
        then every "\" -> "/"
    elsewhere:
        text
```

| Host path | As a program reads it |
| --- | --- |
| `/home/ada/notes` | `/home/ada/notes` |
| `C:\Users\ada\notes` | `C:/Users/ada/notes` |
| `\\?\C:\Users\ada\notes` | `C:/Users/ada/notes` |
| `\\server\share\notes` | `//server/share/notes` |
| `\\?\UNC\server\share\notes` | `//server/share/notes` |

- An extended-length path that is neither a drive path nor a share, a volume
  `\\?\Volume{…}\` among them, MUST keep its `\\?\`: without it the text names
  another place. Its separators are still written `/`.
- The values that go through `of` are `fs.source_dir()`, `fs.working_dir()`
  and `vault.root`, and any absolute path a later proposal gives a program.

### 3. A path below a folder is an identity

- The path of an entry below the folder walked, a card's `path` and an
  `Entry`'s among them, MUST be joined with `/` by the sorted walk itself
  ([[HQL-0020]]). It names a place in a vault and not a file of the host, and
  it is what `group`, `unique`, a join and a diagnostic's `source` read; it is
  the same text wherever the vault is read.
- The runtime opens such an entry by joining it to its folder's host path,
  which Windows reads with either separator. Two paths that differ only in
  their separator compare equal as host paths, so a look-up by path is not
  changed.

### 4. The roots a `Path` knows

`Path`'s operations read the text alone ([[HQL-0040]]), so these hold on every
host, a Windows path read on Linux included.

- A path has one of three roots, or none:

| Root | Written | A path one segment below it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the root of a file system | `/` | `/home` |
| a drive root: a letter, `:` and `/` | `C:/` | `C:/Users` |
| a share: `//`, a server, `/` and a share | `//server/share` | `//server/share/notes` |

- `parent` of a root MUST be `none`.

- `parent` of a path one segment below a root MUST be the root with its `/`:
  `Path("C:/Users").parent()` is `some(Path("C:/"))`, as
  `Path("/home").parent()` is `some(Path("/"))`. `C:` alone names the current
  folder of a drive on Windows, which is another place.
- `name` of a root is the empty text. `join` onto a root adds no second `/`.
- `\` MUST NOT be read as a separator by `Path`'s operations. It is a
  character a file name may hold on Linux and macOS, and the operations do
  not ask which host they run on. A path a program writes with `\`,
  `folder("C:\\data")`, is passed to the host as written and opens the same
  folder on Windows; `parent` and `name` read it as one segment.

### 5. Paths in messages

- A message MUST name a path as the host writes it, so it can be pasted into
  the host's own tools. Where the runtime joins a path it reports, it SHOULD
  join it segment by segment, so one separator is used throughout:
  `project\.hql\config.toml` and not `project\.hql/config.toml`.

## Backwards Compatibility

Nothing changes on Linux and macOS: `of` returns the host's text there, and
the walk joined with `/` already.

On Windows no release carried a binary before this one, so no program built
with one is affected. A program run with a `hql` built from source on Windows
sees `/` where it saw `\`, which is the repair.

The root rules change one result on every host: `Path("C:/Users").parent()`
is `C:/` where it was `C:`.

## Security Considerations

The grants of [[HQL-0040]] compare canonical host paths, and still do: the
containment check canonicalizes both the path asked for and the root, in the
host's form, and never reads the converted text. Converting for display does
not widen what a program may open.

## Reference Implementation

- `src/paths.rs`: `of`, the one conversion, and the text operations with the
  roots of rule 4.
- `src/walk.rs`: `joined`, the join of the sorted walk.
- `src/extensions/fs.rs` and `src/evaluation.rs`: the three values read
  through `of`.
- `src/context.rs`: the project's configuration file joined segment by
  segment.
- `lib/std/io/mod.hql`: the doc string of `parent`, and its reference page.
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and `release.yml`: `windows-latest` beside Linux
  and macOS, with LF kept at checkout.

Not built yet:

- A rule that keeps the next host path from reaching a program without
  `of`. Today it is a convention, and the tests on Windows are what notice.

## Test Plan

Unit tests MUST include:

- every row of the table of rule 2, and a volume path that keeps its `\\?\`;
  the conversion is tested as a function of text, so it runs on every host;
- every row of the table of rule 4, with `parent`, `name` and `join`.

Integration tests, on Windows as on Linux and macOS:

- `the_cards_start_where_wiki_says_and_their_paths_are_relative_to_it` in
  `tests/vault.rs`;
- `walk_is_the_sorted_walk_and_the_vaults_order` and
  `a_relative_path_resolves_against_the_source_directory_of_its_unit` in
  `tests/io_fs.rs`;
- `projects/queries/module-source-dir.hql` in the corpus;
- the page `doc/public/book/21-a-vault.hmd`, whose
  `starts_with("books/")` counts seven cards.

```bash
cargo test --locked
```

## Open Questions

- Should `Path` gain `is_absolute`, now that it knows its roots?
- Should a path written into a file a program produces, a lock file or a
  package manifest, be written in the form of this proposal, so the file is
  the same on every host?

## Changelog

- 2026-11-22: drafted from issue 0082 and built on `feat/win-bin`: the
  conversion, the walk's join, the roots and the messages
